# tools/al/ales_stereo_tone_witness.rish — stereo_tone: the one-pole tone control carried into stereo, the same coefficient shaping both channels — the rung that opens the stereo EQ / filter class (ALES170).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES170.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-074743_fill-ales170-lotus-stereo-tone.md
# Stands on: lotus/tone.rye (ALES40 — the mono one-pole low-pass and high-pass run once per channel, its ToneError reused whole and its precheck sharing one coefficient-and-span validation), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the
# StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the saturate the high-pass complement reuses).
#
# lotus/stereo_tone.rye shapes a stereo master's brightness: it runs ALES40's proven mono one-pole low-pass (and its exact high-pass complement) on BOTH channels with the SAME coefficient, each channel carrying its OWN i64
# state. A tone control is parametric — its coefficient is a smoothing fraction the caller names, not a measured scalar — so the same coefficient on each channel preserves the stereo image for free, while the independent
# per-channel state lets each side filter its own content (linking the state across channels would be a bug, bleeding one channel's transients into the other's brightness). It validates the coefficient and span once against
# the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff on a coefficient outside zero-to-one, BadRange on a span past the samples), so a refusal never filters one channel and leaves the other torn; ToneError reused
# whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is five laws: the stereo low-pass law (each channel equal to ALES40's mono low_pass with the same coefficient byte for byte, the channels
# genuinely differing), the stereo high-pass law (the same for high_pass, with per-channel reconstruction low_pass plus high_pass equal to the input where the difference fits), the parametric / image law (an identical-channel
# master staying identical through both filters, a constant input settling to direct current exactly per channel), the carry law (a span filtered in two pieces, each channel carrying its own state, equal to the whole once on
# both, both states surviving the boundary), and the silence / balance / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_tone_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-tone: Language EN — stereo_tone shapes a stereo master's brightness by running ALES40's proven mono one-pole low-pass (and its exact high-pass complement) on both channels with the same coefficient, each channel carrying its own filter state. A tone control is parametric: its coefficient is a smoothing fraction the caller names, not a measured scalar, so the same coefficient on each channel preserves the stereo image for free, while the independent per-channel state lets each side filter its own content. It validates the coefficient and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never filters one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; and the caller owns each channel's state across calls, so a master filtered in two pieces equals the whole filtered once with no fresh transient at the seam."
say "ales-stereo-tone: Style Radiant — every stereo rung so far carried a level edit, an arrangement edit, a shaping edit, or a dynamics edit into stereo; none changed a master's frequency balance. This rung opens the first stereo filter — a one-pole low-pass that softens a harsh master by rolling off treble on both speakers together, and its exact high-pass complement that thins a boomy one by cutting bass on both — the stereo mirror of the mono tone control ALES40 opened the mono EQ class with, and the rung that opens the stereo EQ and filter class the way ALES129's normalize opened the stereo amplitude class. A tone control is parametric, so the same coefficient on each channel preserves the image for free, the way ALES130's shared ramp did and unlike ALES129's linked scalar or the dynamics family's linked detector, which had to measure across both channels to hold the image. Each channel filters its own content through that shared coefficient: the two states are independent, one per channel, because a filter's state is the running estimate of the signal it is filtering and the left and right signals genuinely differ. That independence is exactly right for equalization — an identical-channel, mono-in-stereo master stays identical because the same input through the same coefficient evolves the same state, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum; linking the state across channels, as the dynamics detector links its envelope, would be a bug here, bleeding one channel's transients into the other's brightness. The crux is five laws: the stereo low-pass law (each channel equal to ALES40's mono low_pass with the same coefficient byte for byte, the two outputs genuinely differing so no channel is crossed); the stereo high-pass law (the same for high_pass, with the per-channel reconstruction of low-pass plus high-pass equal to the input where the difference fits); the parametric and image law (an identical-channel master staying identical through both filters, a constant input settling to direct current exactly on each channel, the low-pass crux carried per channel); the carry law (a span filtered in two pieces, each channel continuing its own ending state, equal to the whole filtered once byte for byte on both, both states surviving the boundary independently); and the silence, balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (an all-silent master filtering to silence on both, the lengths held, a zero denominator, a zero numerator, a coefficient above one, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count zero the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-tone: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying two independent i64 filter states, one estimate step per sample per channel run at full i64 precision so no fractional information is lost between samples and nothing overflows before ALES2's saturate on the high-pass difference; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a filter writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the coefficient and span are validated once through ALES40's precheck against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never filters one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoToneError is ALES40's ToneError (BadCoeff, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault. The stereo low-pass law is proven by running both stereo_low_pass and ALES40's mono low_pass on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the stereo high-pass law the same for high_pass, with the per-channel reconstruction low-pass plus high-pass equal to the input sample for sample where the difference fits; the parametric and image law proves an identical-channel master stays identical through both filters and a constant input settles to direct current exactly on each channel; the carry law proves a span filtered in two pieces, each channel carrying its own state, equal to the whole once on both channels; and the silence, balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the coefficient a smoothing fraction over sample indices rather than a cutoff in hertz, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_tone"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_tone.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-tone: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_tone.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-tone: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-tone: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-tone' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-tone: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-tone line"
